COMMIT a31a26d2a4be7fd886fd45e37798e9be6fea7cf4
docs(ledger): FIX-132 ledger rows made well-formed, by cell shape not pipe count

GFM splits a table cell at any `|` not preceded by a backslash. A row with more
cells than its header loses the excess silently on GitHub -- no error, no visible
gap -- and a row with fewer shifts what follows. Twelve escapes over seven rows
(nine in docs/DECISIONS.md: D-040, D-044, D-053, D-057, D-080; three in
agent/BACKLOG.md: FIX-10, FIX-15), one closing pipe restored on FIX-71, and three
rows rejoined. Every row of both files now splits into exactly its header's cells.

Four review rounds, and all three FAILs were on my sentences rather than on the
change. They are worth the space because the same mistake took three shapes.

The first FAIL: I had claimed the row's own inventory of broken rows was wrong in
five of six entries. It was wrong in two (P1-GATE and P2-01 were well-formed all
along). It was right about FIX-15 and about FIX-54/56/57, and it missed FIX-10 and
FIX-71 -- one of which my own edit that morning had broken.

The second, and the one that matters: I chose the weaker of two available
measurements and then cited it as proof. I counted pipes per row. A row can carry
the right count and the wrong shape -- FIX-54, FIX-56 and FIX-57 each had a stray
separator paid for by a missing closing pipe, seven pipes and seven cells -- and
GitHub had been dropping their entire evidence cell. My check called them clean
and I wrote "nothing is truncated any more" on its strength. The reviewer found
all three. The check is now cell shape: split on unescaped pipes, require the
header's cell count with an empty part at each end.

The third: the FIX-133 row's Done-when cell still demanded the catch form that the
same row retracts two sentences earlier. That cell is what the test-author and the
reviewer hold the task to, so the retraction had not actually taken effect where
it counts. Caught by the reviewer, not by me.

Measured before the change, on `git show HEAD:` copies of both files: 11 rows
deviated and 10 of them lose text -- BACKLOG 5 (FIX-10 at 8 cells, FIX-15 at 7,
FIX-54/56/57 at 7 each) and DECISIONS 5 (D-040 and D-057 at 6, D-044/053/080 at
5). The 11th, FIX-71, had six cells and no closing pipe, and GFM makes the
trailing pipe optional, so that row rendered correctly: cosmetic, not broken.
After: 0 deviating, BACKLOG 260/260 and DECISIONS 113/113 (81 four-cell rows plus
32 legacy rows with no Reason cell, left alone by intent).

Evidence that no decision's words changed, attributed because the two halves have
different authors: `git diff --cached --word-diff=porcelain docs/DECISIONS.md`
filtered to non-pipe tokens returns exactly one hunk, the D-111 amendment -- run
by me. The reviewer has no shell and compared the -/+ pairs by eye instead; that
reading is his and he said so both times.

Riding along in this commit, stated rather than hidden:

- An amendment to D-111 in docs/DECISIONS.md, which belongs to FIX-133. D-111 told
  the implementer to widen Recorder's catch to `RuntimeException | Error`, copying
  D-104's form from BatchingWriter. That is a NARROWING here: the existing
  `catch (Exception)` deliberately catches the checked InterruptedException that
  D-056's stop-deadline cancel throws sneakily, so the prescribed form would have
  deleted the FAILED row for every cancelled delivery in order to add one for an
  Error. The form is `Throwable`; `Exception | Error` would also close the hole but
  only for a reader who already knows a direct Throwable subclass cannot reach the
  line. D-104's multicatch style stands where its code cannot throw a checked
  exception at all. Caught by me, before any code was written, and the existing
  test aCancelledStepRecordsItsFailureSnapshot would have caught it otherwise.
- agent/packets/FIX-133.md, the packet the corrected row points at.
- FIX-139, a new row: a CI guard asserting cell shape on both ledgers. Its
  done-when requires a FIX-54-shaped red case, because a pipe-counting guard would
  wave that one through, which is the whole lesson here.

agent/HANDOFF.md carries the integration evidence for the Java tree at e65f616,
observed by the verifier on the full reactor: `clean verify -Pit` exit 0, BUILD
SUCCESS, 12:56, 17 SUCCESS lines, 0 failures, errors or skips, no blocked platform
thread; ci/coverage_check.py exit 0, "coverage: no decrease", api 99.3 line /
100.0 branch, core 99.6 / 99.5, runtime 99.5 / 99.5, lowest module bench 96.8 /
95.4. That measures FIX-71 plus the two cherry-picked lane3 commits. It says
nothing about this commit, which is markdown only and cannot reach a build.

