 | D-108 | 2026-11-14 | Shape of the one-id lifecycle (D-107's design, class-C). Draft — review at hand-over. **Keying:** `messages` gains `attempt int not null default 1` and its PK becomes `(id, attempt, step)`, inserted `on conflict (id, attempt, step) do nothing` so the first write of each run still wins; `Snapshot` gains `attempt`, read by `Recorder` from `Envelope.ATTEMPT`; `TIMELINE` orders by `attempt, step`. One monotonic run number per id and never two counters: a worker run is `attempt_base + claims - 1`, a resend run is `parked.attempt + 1`, and `ATTEMPT` stays the single source of `nextKey`'s `part~N`, so `BlobKey`'s arity does not change and the 321 factory calls in tests stand. `bodies`, `message_keys`, `work` and `processed_ids` need no migration. **Lifecycle:** the current state is **derived, never a column** (the batching writer is insert-only, so a state transition would have to update N rows): cancelled if a cancel event exists, else failed if a DLQ row or a held work item exists, else the latest attempt's last row. New append-only table `message_lifecycle(id, seq, at, event, attempt, actor, reason)`, PK `(id, seq)`, events `resent` and `cancelled` only — run outcomes stay in `messages` — with the PK collision itself as the concurrency guard, so of two simultaneous cancels one wins. core `MessageLog` interface (in-memory + Pg on day 1) and core `Lifecycle(Dlq, WorkQueue, MessageLog)`. **Cancel:** append the event first, then delete the DLQ row, then complete a held item — the lifecycle row is the authority, so a crash after it leaves a stale DLQ row that reads render as cancelled. **Retry:** `Replay` becomes `Retry` with one entry point `retry(messageId)`, same id, attempt `parked.attempt + 1`, appending `resent` **before** the run and deleting the DLQ row only on success; no id is minted anywhere. **DLQ:** PK stays `id` (no PK migration), gains `attempt`, and park becomes `on conflict (id) do update ... where excluded.attempt > dlq.attempt` — the DLQ is the worklist of the latest failure, the history is `messages` + `message_lifecycle`. **Removed:** both `Replay.from` overloads, `REPLAY_INPUT`, the refusal reasons STEP_OUT_OF_RANGE / NOT_RECORDED / METADATA_ONLY, `Replay`'s `IdGenerator` and its wiring, the replay admin route, and `Envelope.REPLAY_OF` — `replayOf` does not survive, because a retry under the same id has nothing to point at. **Migration** V12, one file, including dropping and recreating `messages_created_at` as `(created_at, id, attempt, step)` and changing the retention job's keyset cursor. | Class-C design 2026-11-14 on D-107, accepted by me as the architect with three of its open questions answered here and two put to the owner. Rejected alternatives and why, from the design: a current-state column on `messages` (insert-only writer, N rows per transition); a separate `runs` table (`messages` already is the per-run log once keyed by attempt); DLQ PK `(id, attempt)` (turns the worklist into a history and makes `get`/`list` need max-attempt windows for no gain); a two-dimension `part~G.A` suffix (breaks `ComponentContract.java:320-321` and keeps two counters); keeping the new id with a parent link (the owner's direction forbids it); cancel by interrupting a running message (D-103: an interrupt is a shutdown signal, never a failure of the message); cancel as a prop on existing rows (mutates snapshots). My answers to the design's open questions: `actor` is nullable and written null until the admin API has authentication, which it does not have today; the number of resends per id takes a finite cap per flow (SUPERSEDED the same day by D-109: the owner refused a cap and was right, and that row says why my reading of "never default a limit to unlimited" was wrong here); and a resend of an ordered flow must go back through the queue rather than run inline, because an inline resend jumps the order its flow guarantees — that is correctness, not preference, and it is what needs `work.attempt_base`. |
 | D-109 | 2026-11-14 | What a resend may and may not do (owner, closing D-108's open questions). **No cap on resends**: an operator may resend the same message id any number of times, and each resend is a further attempt with its own rows. **A sync message is never resendable**: a sync request/reply flow parks nothing (§4), the error went to the caller, and the caller is the one who retries — `Retry.retry` refuses a message whose flow is sync, naming the flow. **A cancelled message is never resendable**: cancel is terminal, so once a `cancelled` event exists in the message's log, retry is refused. The lifecycle is therefore `failed -> resent -> ok` or `failed -> resent -> failed`, any number of times, or `failed -> cancelled`, which ends it. | Owner's decision 2026-11-14 ("ne, žádný strop přeposílání nechci, sync zprávy nepújde přepossílat a zrušené zprávy taky ne"). On the cap the owner overruled me and was right: I had called for a finite cap in D-108 on the strength of "never default a limit to unlimited", and I had over-applied that rule. It guards limits the platform enforces on itself while running — body size, retries, concurrency, deadlines — where an unbounded value is a runaway. A resend is a human action taken one click at a time; there is no loop to run away, each one is recorded in the message's log with its actor, and storage is already bounded by the retention job. A cap would have bought nothing and would have had to be explained to an operator at the worst possible moment. The two refusals are preconditions on one entry point and cost nothing: the flow's delivery mode is known at retry time because the flow is deployed, and the cancel event is a single read of `message_lifecycle`. |
